# CoolWebSearch (2003-2006). A browser hijacker that changed the home page and search and kept coming back.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee is an office coordinator finishing the staff newsletter. Dana approves a print proof safely,
# then a colleague's tip for "free cursors" leads to a download page, and CoolWebSearch takes over the browser.
# The victim is invented: the hijacker hit office and home users alike, mostly people who wanted free screensavers and cursors.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | CoolWebSearch was not one program but a family of them. It got in through browser flaws and "free" downloads such as cursors and screensavers, often with no window at all.
  | It then took over the home page, the search page and the error pages, opened pop-ups, edited the hosts file so security sites would not load, and put itself back when removed.
  | Dana and the company are invented: the hijacker hit anyone who went looking for something free to make their screen more fun.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, office coordinator at Northwind Traders. Once a month you put together the staff newsletter, and today is deadline day.
  | It is early afternoon. You have finished 3 of the 5 newsletter jobs. Your browser opens on the Northwind intranet, as it always does. Two jobs to go.
  | IT has paused updates on this PC until the newsletter software is certified, and left a note on the desktop. That is a bad habit, and it is why this browser has no recent fixes.
quota 3 5 Newsletter jobs done today

# Dana's desk: a demo convenience, so a soft flag.
file Desktop/it-note.txt
  at 930 200
  | IT note (training copy)
  | Windows and browser updates are paused on this PC until the newsletter software is certified.

# The browser as it should be: it opens on the company intranet.
page intranet intranet.northwind.example
  title Northwind intranet
  brand Northwind Traders
  favorite
  heading Northwind intranet
  | Welcome back, Dana.
  section Today
  row Newsletter deadline = Friday, 4 pm
  row Printer proofs = printco.example
  row Staff offsite = Hartley Hall
homepage intranet
open intranet

# The safe round: the print shop's proof, on its own address.
page proof-ok printco.example/proofs/NW-212/approved
  title Proof approved
  brand PrintCo Proofs
  heading Proof approved
  | Newsletter NW-212 goes to print this evening. Delivery tomorrow morning.
page proof-changes printco.example/proofs/NW-212/changes
  title Changes requested
  brand PrintCo Proofs
  heading Changes requested
  | We will send a new proof within the hour.
page proof printco.example/proofs/NW-212
  title Approve proof: NW-212
  brand PrintCo Proofs
  heading Approve proof: NW-212
  | The proof of the staff newsletter is ready for your approval.
  section Proof
  row Job = Northwind staff newsletter, November
  row Pages = 4, full colour
  row Copies = 120
  row Delivery = Northwind Traders, front desk
  button Approve proof -> proof-ok
  button Ask for changes -> proof-changes
mail mail-proof
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Newsletter job 4 of 5: approve the print proof
  link proof
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | PrintCo has sent the proof of the newsletter. The link goes straight to it: look it over and approve it.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Newsletter job 4 of 5: approve the print proof

step The link goes to the print shop
  look Mail: Priya's message with the proof link
  when mail mail-proof
  | Priya has sent the print shop's proof of the newsletter. Approve it the usual way, so you know what normal looks like.
  | The email links to the print shop's own address, printco.example, which Northwind has used for years. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open mail-proof

step Check the proof
  look the job, the pages and the copies
  when visit proof
  move Check the job and the number of copies, then click “Approve proof”.
  | The proof page is short: the job, the pages, the copies and two buttons. Nothing to install and nothing to sign in to.
open proof

step Job 4 of 5 done
  look the counter in the guide: one job left, or so it seems
  when choose proof any
  | Approved. Four jobs done. And another email has just arrived: Marcus has found something for the newsletter.
choose proof 1
quota +1

# The lure: a colleague's tip for free cursors, and a page that offers a program.
view cws-installer message
  title Smiley Cursors Plus
  app Smiley Cursors Plus
  icon info
  | Thank you for installing Smiley Cursors Plus.
  button OK -> close primary
program cws SmileyCursorsPlus-setup
  publisher none
  signed no
  prompt open-warning
  description Free cursors and screensavers
  process msconfd.exe
  cpu 5
  mem 21
  parent explorer.exe
  command C:\Windows\System32\msconfd.exe
  startup msconfd C:\Windows\System32\msconfd.exe
  location HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
  view cws-installer
page cws-lure free-cursors.invalid
  title Free cursors and screensavers
  brand Free Cursors
  heading Smiley Cursors Plus
  | Free animated cursors and screensavers. Make your PC fun! Click Download, then run the file.
  section Preview
  item Bouncing smiley pointer
  item Rainbow trail
  item Falling snow screensaver
  section Download
  row File = SmileyCursorsPlus-setup.exe
  row Size = 209 KB
  row Cost = Free
  button Download SmileyCursorsPlus-setup.exe
mail mail-cursors
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Free cursors for the newsletter
  link cws-lure
  | Dana,
  |
  | For the newsletter's last page, I found a site with free animated cursors and screensavers. My PC looks great with them. Try the link.
  |
  | Marcus
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Free cursors for the newsletter

step A tip from Marcus
  look Mail: Marcus's message with a link to a free-download site
  when mail mail-cursors
  | Marcus is a real colleague and means well, so the email itself is fine. The link goes to a site Dana has never used.
  | The link does not go to Northwind or to the print shop. Look at the address before you click it.
open mail-cursors

step Free cursors
  look the page: the Download section and the file name in it
  when visit cws-lure
  move Read the Download section under the preview, then click “Download SmileyCursorsPlus-setup.exe”.
  | The page offers a small program. Cursors are pictures: they should not need a program to run.
  | Many victims never got this far: on an unpatched browser the page could install CoolWebSearch by itself.
open cws-lure

step The program arrives
  look the new file in Downloads
  when choose cws-lure 1
  | The download finished and left a program in Downloads. Nothing has run yet.
file Downloads/SmileyCursorsPlus-setup.exe
  program cws
  size 214016
notify browser Download complete
  | SmileyCursorsPlus-setup.exe is in your Downloads folder.
open Downloads

step The warning almost nobody reads
  look the warning: a program from an unknown publisher
  when prompt cws
  move Double-click “SmileyCursorsPlus-setup” in Downloads.
  | When the file is opened, the computer asks whether to run a program from an unknown publisher. This warning was the last chance to stop it.
open Downloads/SmileyCursorsPlus-setup.exe

step It installs and hides
  look the browser's new home page, and the activity chips: msconfd.exe and the start-up entry
  when run cws
  | A short thank-you box and nothing else. In the background it starts msconfd.exe, a name made to look like part of Windows, and adds itself to the start-up list so it starts every time Dana signs in.
  | The home page and the search page are changed now too: the browser jumps to a page nobody asked for. Another email has come in: the last job.
# The browser is Dana's no more. A dead venue link lands on the hijacker's results instead of the browser's error page.
page cws coolwebsearch.invalid
  title CoolWebSearch
  heading CoolWebSearch
  toolbar CoolBar
  | Search the web
  section Hot links
  tone injected
  item Casino bonus
  item Pharmacy online
  item Fast loans
  item Free screensavers
  note Sponsored results. This is a training copy.
page venue hartley-hall.example/directions-2025
  title CoolWebSearch: did you mean
  heading Did you mean one of these?
  toolbar CoolBar
  | The page you asked for could not be found, so here are sponsored results instead.
  section Sponsored results
  tone injected
  item Casino bonus
  item Pharmacy online
  item Fast loans
  note Sponsored results. This is a training copy.
  button Search again
homepage cws
open cws
answer run
mail mail-venue
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Newsletter job 5 of 5: offsite directions
  link venue
  | Dana,
  |
  | Last job: the offsite page needs the directions and parking for Hartley Hall. I have pasted the link from last year's offsite newsletter, which goes to the venue's own website.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Newsletter job 5 of 5: offsite directions

step Job 5 of 5
  look Mail: Priya's message about the offsite directions
  when mail mail-venue
  | The last newsletter job needs the venue's directions. Priya's link goes to the venue's own website.
open mail-venue

step The venue's page is not the venue's
  look the address bar and the page: a "Did you mean" list, not the venue's directions
  when visit venue
  move Look at the page, then click “Search again”. Flag anything that looks wrong.
  | The venue's page is gone, so the old link is dead. A dead link should show the browser's own "page not found" page.
  | Instead, the browser shows a CoolWebSearch results list with a toolbar nobody asked for. Nothing else looks broken, only different.
open venue

# Pop-ups that come back: Close on the second one reopens the first, and the browser settings are locked.
view cws-popup-1 message
  title Message from the web
  app Browser
  icon warning
  | You have been selected! Claim your free prize now.
  | This is a training copy of the kind of pop-up CoolWebSearch opened.
  button Claim now -> none primary
  button Close -> close
view cws-popup-2 message
  title Hot links
  app Browser
  icon info
  | Cheap pills, casino bonus, fast loans.
  | A pop-up that opens again and again.
  button Open -> none primary
  button Close -> done
view cws-options panel
  title Browser settings: Home page
  app Browser
  icon gear
  heading Home page and search
  section On start
  row Home page = http://coolwebsearch.invalid
  row Search engine = CoolWebSearch
  note This setting is greyed out and set by a program on this PC. Changing it does not stick.
  button Use a blank page -> done
  button Close -> close primary

step Pop-ups on their own
  look the two pop-up windows
  when choose venue any
  move Close both pop-ups with their Close button.
  | The search does nothing useful, and pop-ups open on their own, even though no page asked for them.
show cws-popup-1
show cws-popup-2

step They come back
  look the browser settings window, and the pop-up that opened again
  when done cws-popup-2
  move In the settings window, click “Use a blank page”.
  | Closing a pop-up does not end them: the program opens another. Dana opens the browser's settings to put the home page back, and finds the setting greyed out.
show cws-popup-1
show cws-options

step It will not go away
  look the hosts copy in Documents, and the browser's home page: CoolWebSearch again
  when done cws-options
  | Dana sets a blank page. It looks as if it worked, then a hidden program writes coolwebsearch.invalid back within minutes. The newsletter is finished, and the browser is not Dana's any more.
  | The hosts copy in Documents shows why: the security sites point at the hijacker's address, so the cleanup tools cannot load. Dana gives up and phones the venue for the directions.
close cws-popup-1
close cws-options
open cws
file Documents/hosts.txt
  | Copy of C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts (training copy)
  | 203.0.113.7 security-vendor.example
  | 203.0.113.7 www.security-vendor.example
open Documents
quota +1

step What CoolWebSearch did
  look the animation: the home page, the searches, the pop-ups
  | For years this family took over the home page, the search and the error pages of anyone who ran one "free" program or visited one unpatched page.
  | It watched the searches and the mistyped addresses, and sold that traffic as advertising.
  | Nothing was locked. The harm was the control, and how hard it was to give back: removal tools often had to be run in Safe Mode.
attack spyware-surveillance
  | the home page
  | the search page
  | pop-ups
  | security sites blocked
  | reinstalled itself
block 203.0.113.7 80

flag hard link mail-cursors
  | A tip pointing to a free-download site nobody at Northwind uses, not to the company or the print shop. Check where a link goes before you click.
flag hard section cws-lure 2
  label The Download box: a setup program for cursors
  | The page offers a setup program for cursors. Cursors are pictures and need no installer.
flag hard dialog open-warning cws
  | Windows said the publisher was unknown and the file was a program, not a picture.
flag hard process msconfd.exe
  | CoolWebSearch running in the background, under a name that looks like part of Windows.
flag hard startup msconfd
  | It added itself to the programs that start every time Windows does.
flag hard section venue 1
  label The venue's address showing sponsored links
  | A dead link showed sponsored results instead of the browser's own "page not found" page.
flag hard section cws 1
  label The new home page
  | A home page nobody chose, full of sponsored links, with a toolbar nobody installed.
flag hard file Documents/hosts.txt
  label The hosts file copy: security sites sent to another address
  | The hosts file sends the security sites to the hijacker's address, so the cleanup sites cannot load.
flag soft file Desktop/it-note.txt
  | Switching off updates leaves the browser flaws CoolWebSearch used wide open. On a real PC, keep updates on.

lesson
  | Keep the system and the browser up to date: most of these installed through flaws that updates had already fixed.
  | A friendly tip can still lead to a bad site. Do not run free-download programs from unknown publishers; a pointer or screensaver never needs to run as a program. Windows warns you for a reason.
  | A dead or mistyped address should show the browser's own "page not found" page. Sponsored results instead mean a program changed your browser.
  | If the home page keeps changing back, a program is resetting it. Do not only change the setting: run a cleanup tool or ask IT.
